# Lands at: /etc/crypttab   (one added line; the stock commented examples and
#                            the "do not list your root here" note stay)
#
# NEW. There was no crypttab entry on the Ventoy-era install, because the only
# encrypted volume was p4 and that is opened by the initramfs from
# rd.luks.name= on the kernel cmdline, never from crypttab. p3 is new: it has
# to be opened by the RUNNING system, late, so that kernel and mkinitcpio
# updates regenerate the UKI in the place the loader actually reads.
#
# How it gets unlocked, given p3 and p4 share one passphrase.
#
# Not from the passphrase. The passphrase reaches the initramfs as an sd-stub
# credential (DESIGN Section 5.1), systemd-cryptsetup consumes it to open p4,
# and the initramfs is discarded at the pivot. Whether that credential is
# still readable in the real root afterwards was NOT verified while writing
# this - it depends on whether PID 1 materialises stub credentials under
# /run/credentials/@system and whether that survives switch-root - so nothing
# here is built on it. If you want to check, look for
# /run/credentials/@system/cryptsetup.passphrase on a booted system; if it is
# there, a simpler crypttab is possible and this keyfile can go.
#
# Instead: a second p3 keyslot holding a random keyfile that lives on the
# already-decrypted root. This is the ordinary "unlock a secondary volume from
# the encrypted root" pattern, it needs no passphrase and no prompt, and it
# gives back exactly the property the old FAT /boot had - the shelf is simply
# mounted, all the time, so every pacman hook just works.
#
# It gives away nothing. The keyfile sits inside a filesystem that is already
# encrypted under the same passphrase that opens p3, so an attacker who can
# read the keyfile has already opened the volume it unlocks. DESIGN Section
# 7.5 names this concentration as an accepted residual; this does not add to
# it.
#
# Create the slot with argon2id (cryptsetup's default - do NOT pass
# --pbkdf pbkdf2 here) and 512 bits of entropy:
#
#     install -d -m700 /etc/cryptsetup-keys.d
#     ( umask 077; head -c 64 /dev/urandom > /etc/cryptsetup-keys.d/cryptefi.key )
#     cryptsetup luksAddKey /dev/disk/by-partuuid/<p3-partuuid> \
#         /etc/cryptsetup-keys.d/cryptefi.key
#
# argon2id specifically, and this is checked against the loader rather than
# assumed: src/luks/luks.c marks a keyslot usable only when its kdf type is
# pbkdf2, and luks_unlock tries the passphrase against every usable slot,
# returning LUKS_ERR_UNSUPPORTED_KDF only when none existed. So an argon2id
# slot is skipped outright and costs the in-EFI unlock nothing, whereas a
# second pbkdf2 slot at mkstick's 200000 iterations would make the loader burn
# a full extra derivation on a slot it can never open. The keyfile has full
# entropy, so the weaker-looking choice is also the free one.
#
# discard, for the same reason p4 has rd.luks.options=...=discard: without it
# dm-crypt swallows every discard and fstrim on /efi does nothing.
# nofail, so a p3 that will not open cannot hold up the boot.
#
# The device is addressed by LUKS UUID rather than PARTUUID to match the form
# /etc/kernel/cmdline uses for p4. Read it with:
#     cryptsetup luksUUID /dev/disk/by-partuuid/<p3-partuuid>

# <name>       <device>                                     <password>                             <options>
cryptefi       UUID=NEW_P3_LUKS_UUID                        /etc/cryptsetup-keys.d/cryptefi.key     luks,discard,nofail
